WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.507 --> 00:00:04.340
(singing in foreign language)

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.600
- I never thought in a million years

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.210
that I would be here in Japan singing

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.010
the music that my
grandmother introduced me to.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.550
I'm a testament to say that no matter

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:19.203
how big your dreams are, they
can definitely be achieved.

00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:30.100
(speaking foreign language)

00:00:36.359 --> 00:00:38.390
(singing in foreign language)

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.240
Enka is a form of Japanese music,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.310
so it's only sung in Japan.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.490
It is considered a older genre of music.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.490
I call it Japanese blues because

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.090
all of the lyrics are
very sad, melodramatic.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:54.140
They often talk about
lost loves and heartbreak.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.940
It uses a lot of imagination.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.220
It really doesn't spell out the
meaning of the song for you,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.960
so everyone who listens to the music

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.143
can have their own interpretation.

00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:07.270
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.530
I was born and raised in
Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.560
My grandmother, she was
originally from Yokohama.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.830
I basically went to my grandmother's house

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.370
just about every weekend.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:19.150
She listened to enka music all the time.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.840
I think it was a way for her
to keep Japan in her heart,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.700
even while she was living in the U.S.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.890
I started singing around five years old.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.480
My grandmother would always
say, "You sing so well."

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:31.640
Just to see a smile on her face

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.100
would just brighten my day
because I loved her so much.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.170
Because I had so much love for enka

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.540
and wanted to know what the
lyrics I was singing meant,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.870
I started studying Japanese in high school

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.433
and throughout college.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.490
I was always interested in Japan

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.600
and where my mom and my
grandmother came from.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.913
So I moved to Japan to
become an enka singer.

00:01:55.287 --> 00:01:58.780
(singing in foreign language)

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.500
The very first time I stood on the stage

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.010
and sang for a crowd was actually on TV.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.130
Kinda like American Idol.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.060
I was given so much praise and

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.110
it made me feel really, really good.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.960
They were just so surprised.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.827
Like, why would would a 20-something American

00:02:13.827 --> 00:02:16.460
be listening to a genre like this?

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.160
And it kinda worked in my favor.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.380
(singing in foreign language)

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.350
I started singing professionally in 2008.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.970
There's never been an
American whose been on stage

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.060
singing enka professionally here in Japan.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.700
I've had the opportunity to
do different types of songs.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.435
Children's songs, musicals, TV dramas.

00:02:36.435 --> 00:02:38.966
I think I've been really embraced

00:02:38.966 --> 00:02:42.040
wholeheartedly from the Japanese audience.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.190
Me singing enka really was a way for me

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.570
to connect to Japan and
connect with my grandmother.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.778
And to this day, the love I
have for enka has not changed.

00:02:51.778 --> 00:02:55.778
(speaking in foreign language)

